=== SIGNATURE ===
streak(arr)

=== COMMENT ===
For each cell, a running count of consecutive equal values in row-major order: 1 when the value differs from the previous item, otherwise the previous count plus 1. Output is the same size and shape as arr.

=== CODE ===
=LAMBDA(arr,
  LET(
    cols, COLUMNS(arr),
    flat, TOCOL(arr),
    n_items, ROWS(flat),
    same_as_prev, MAP(SEQUENCE(n_items), LAMBDA(i,
      IF(i = 1, FALSE, INDEX(flat, i) = INDEX(flat, i - 1)))),
    run_len, SCAN(0, same_as_prev, LAMBDA(acc, is_same, IF(is_same, acc + 1, 1))),
    WRAPROWS(run_len, cols)
  )
)

=== DESCRIPTION ===
Counts consecutive runs of equal values. Reading arr in row-major order (left to right, top to bottom), each cell is 1 when its value differs from the item just before it, and one more than the previous count when it matches - so the number tells you how many of that value have appeared in a row so far. The returned array has the same size and shape as arr.

Arguments:
  arr - a single value or a 1D/2D array.

Returns:
  An array the same size and shape as arr of run-length counts (each run restarts at 1).

How it works: the input is flattened row-major, each item is compared with the one before it, and SCAN turns that "same as previous" flag into a running counter that resets to 1 whenever the value changes. The counts are wrapped back into arr's shape with WRAPROWS.

Numbers and text are compared exactly (5 and "5" are different). Blank cells read as 0.

Examples:
  streak({"A";"B";"C";"C";"D";"D";"D";"E"})  ->  {1;1;1;2;1;2;3;1}
  streak({"A","A";"B","A"})                  ->  {1,2;1,1}
